 "reading_text": "The user is asking about a gross pathology image showing an abdominal cavity opened at autopsy. I need to look carefully at the image features.\n\nWhat I see: an opened abdominal cavity (laparotomy/autopsy view), loops of bowel (small intestine) that appear grayish-purple, congested, somewhat edematous. There's a greenish-gray substance on the serosal surfaces and surrounding the bowel, with what looks like fibrin exudate — a shaggy, yellowish-white/grayish coating on the peritoneal surfaces and between loops. There's also dark fluid (blood or bile?) in places.\n\nFibrinous peritonitis is characterized by exudation of fibrin onto the peritoneal surfaces, giving a \"villous\" or shaggy, grayish-yellow appearance, often with fibrin strands between loops of bowel. In the image, the right side shows a pale greenish-gray, somewhat shaggy coating on the serosa/omentum, consistent with fibrinous exudate. The bowel loops look matted.\n\n",
